{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794073868306,"sessionID":"ses_e48de3ad0ffeE5McT5ooWqWS7z","part":{"partID":"prt_1b722b4c7001STwgdZfi7WPkfu","sessionID":"ses_e48de3ad0ffeE5McT5ooWqWS7z","messageID":"msg_1b721c9ba001Je2RrZrEeSojq1","type":"tool","id":"call_c2f0fc32fd98435a9f461605","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md, lines 1-346\n1: ---\n2: name: deep-review\n3: description: \"Autonomous iterative code-review loop with externalized state, convergence detection, P0/P1/P2 findings, fresh context per pass.\"\n4: argument-hint: \"[target] [:auto|:confirm] [--max-iterations=N] [--convergence=N] [--stop-policy=convergence|max-iterations]\"\n5: version: 1.11.14.0\n6: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, Task]\n7: ---\n8: <!-- Note: Task is for the command executor (loop management); @deep-review agent is LEAF-only (no Task). No WebFetch: review is code-only. -->\n9: \n10: # Autonomous Deep Review Loop\n11: \n12: Iterative code review and quality auditing protocol with fresh context per iteration, externalized state, convergence detection, and severity-weighted findings (P0/P1/P2).\n13: \n14: Runtime path resolution:\n15: - OpenCode/Copilot runtime: `.opencode/agents/*.md`\n16: - Claude runtime: `.claude/agents/*.md`\n17: \n18: Convergence threshold semantics and sibling-parity notes (deep-review 0.10 vs deep-research 0.05 vs deep-ai-council 0.20) live in `references/convergence/convergence.md` §1 under \"Threshold Semantics and Sibling Parity\".\n19: \n20: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n21: \n22: ### When to Use This Skill\n23: \n24: Use this skill when:\n25: - Code quality audit requiring multiple rounds across different review dimensions\n26: - Spec folder validation requiring cross-reference checks between docs and implementation\n27: - Release readiness check before shipping a feature or component\n28: - Finding misalignments between spec documents and actual code\n29: - Verifying cross-references across documentation, agents, commands, and code\n30: - Iterative review where each dimension's findings inform subsequent dimensions\n31: - Unattended or overnight audit sessions\n32: \n33: ### When NOT to Use\n34: \n35: - Simple single-pass code review (use `sk-code`'s code-review mode instead)\n36: - Known issues that just need fixing (go directly to implementation)\n37: - Implementation tasks (use `sk-code` or `/speckit:implement`)\n38: - Quick one-file checks (use direct Grep/Read)\n39: - Fewer than 2 review dimensions needed (single-pass suffices)\n40: \n41: ### FORBIDDEN INVOCATION PATTERNS\n42: \n43: This skill is invoked EXCLUSIVELY through the `/deep:review` command. The command's YAML workflow owns state, dispatch, and convergence.\n44: \n45: **NEVER:**\n46: - Write a custom bash/shell dispatcher to parallelize iterations (ad-hoc shell fan-out)\n47: - Invoke any CLI executor (cli-codex, cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-cursor, cli-devin, cli-pi, cli-deepseek-harness) directly in a loop to simulate iterations\n48: - Manually write iteration prompts to `/tmp` and dispatch them via `copilot -p`\n49: - Dispatch the `@deep-review` LEAF agent via the Task tool for iteration loops (the agent is LEAF, a single iteration, and MUST be driven by the command's workflow)\n50: - Skip the state machine: `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-config.json`, `deltas/`, `prompts/`, `logs/`\n51: - Manage iteration state outside the resolved local review packet under `{spec_folder}/review/`\n52: \n53: **COMMAND-DRIVEN FAN-OUT IS SUPPORTED:** use `--executor`/`--executors`/`--concurrency` flags on `/deep:review`. The command's YAML `step_fanout_spawn` owns multi-lineage dispatch; `fanout-merge.cjs` applies strongest-restriction (any lineage active P0 → merged FAIL). This is not ad-hoc shell dispatch — it is the canonical fan-out path. Intra-lineage wave orchestration remains deferred.\n54: \n55: **ALWAYS:**\n56: - Invoke via `/deep:review :auto` or `/deep:review :confirm`\n57: - Let the command's YAML workflow own dispatch (auto: `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml`)\n58: - Record each leaf iteration through the append gateway (`runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --mode review --run-directory <spec folder> --event-json <file>`) — the gateway is the SINGLE state-log writer: it authorizes, fences, and receipts the write, then refreshes `{state_paths.state_log}` from the ledger\n59: - Treat `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` as the reducer for DERIVED artifacts only (finding registry, dashboard metrics, strategy updates); it never writes the state log\n60: - Require every iteration to produce BOTH the markdown narrative AND the JSONL delta (dispatch scripts must fail if either is missing)\n61: - Use `resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review')` from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/shared/review-research-paths.cjs` to locate the canonical review root\n62: \n63: ### Trigger Phrases\n64: \n65: - \"review code quality\" / \"audit this code\"\n66: - \"audit spec folder\" / \"validate spec completeness\"\n67: - \"release readiness check\" / \"pre-release review\"\n68: - \"find misalignments\" (between spec and implementation)\n69: - \"verify cross-references\" (across docs and code)\n70: - \"deep review\" / \"iterative review\" / \"review loop\"\n71: - \"quality audit\" / \"convergence detection\"\n72: \n73: ### Keyword Triggers\n74: \n75: `deep review`, `convergence review`, `iterative review`, `review loop`, `release readiness`, `spec folder review`, `convergence detection`, `quality audit`, `find misalignments`, `verify cross-references`, `pre-release review`, `audit spec folder`\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n80: \n81: The `system-deep-loop` hub routes an iterative review loop here, and its `review` mode signal selects this packet.\n82: The signal fires when a request names a deep review, a convergence review, a review loop, a release readiness check or a spec-folder audit.\n83: The loop runs only through `/deep:review`, whose workflow owns state, dispatch and convergence.\n84: \n85: `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` loads first on every invocation.\n86: The review phase named by the dispatch context then selects the loop protocol, the convergence documents or the state documents.\n87: \n88: The routing pseudocode in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) is the one route from a request to a resource set.\n89: A request whose intent scores stay under the threshold returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with a disambiguation checklist, never a silent default.\n90: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n95: \n96: ### Architecture\n97: \n98: `/deep:review` owns the loop. The YAML workflow initializes state, dispatches one LEAF review iteration at a time, evaluates convergence, synthesizes `review-report.md`, and saves continuity. The LEAF agent reads state, reviews one dimension, writes `iteration-NNN.md`, updates strategy, and appends JSONL. Full 4-phase lifecycle: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n99: \n100: ### Resource Map Coverage Gate\n101: \n102: When `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init, deep review treats it as a mandatory audit input and does four things:\n103: \n104: - Persists `resource_map_present` in config.\n105: - Seeds `Known Context` with a snapshot of the map.\n106: - Has at least one iteration audit `target_files` coverage against the map: touched, not-touched with `expected-by-scope`-versus-`gap`, and absent-from-map, with findings tagged `resource-map-coverage`.\n107: - Has synthesis add the `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` report section and a converged `resource-map.md`. Disable this with `--no-resource-map`.\n108: \n109: Absent at init, the gate and section are skipped without failing the loop.\n110: Full sequencing: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` (init 9a, iteration 3b, synthesis 4). Schema: `references/state/state-format.md`.\n111: \n112: ### Bounded Context Snapshot Replacement\n113: \n114: During initialization, capture a bounded, pointer-based context snapshot in `deep-review-strategy.md` `Known Context` before the first dimension runs.\n115: The snapshot holds:\n116: \n117: - Target pointers: files, specs, symbols and resource-map entries.\n118: - The claimed behavior and acceptance criteria to verify.\n119: - Reuse and convention pointers.\n120: - Risk areas, missing context, stale-graph caveats and out-of-scope areas.\n121: \n122: Do not create a context-loop report or widen scope beyond the declared target.\n123: Use `@context` for quick retrieval, and `/speckit:plan` only when findings require implementation planning.\n124: \n125: ### State Packet Location\n126: \n127: The review state packet always lives under the target spec's local `review/` folder.\n128: Root-spec targets use `{spec_folder}/review/` directly.\n129: Child-phase and sub-phase targets use **flat-first** placement: the first run writes flat, and a `pt-NN` sibling packet is allocated only when prior content already exists for a different target.\n130: Full resolution rule and worked examples: `references/state/state-format.md` §1.\n131: \n132: Core artifacts: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-strategy.md`, `deep-review-dashboard.md`, `.deep-review-pause`, `resource-map.md`, `review-report.md`, and `iterations/iteration-NNN.md`.\n133: \n134: The loop writes `review/review-report.md` itself at synthesis rather than scaffolding it through `create.sh --level review`.\n135: The report is assembled from the findings registry and the JSONL state, so its content exists only once the loop has run, and a scaffolded skeleton would be overwritten whole.\n136: `create.sh --level review` exists for a review packet authored by hand, and its template carries the same nine sections the loop emits.\n137: \n138: ### Core Innovation: Fresh Context Per Iteration\n139: \n140: Each agent dispatch gets a fresh context window; state continuity comes from files, not memory, preventing accumulated findings from biasing later dimensions. Init writes config/strategy/JSONL; each loop reads state, checks convergence, dispatches one dimension, records findings, and reduces state; synthesis compiles the report and saves continuity.\n141: \n142: ### Review Dimensions\n143: \n144: The four primary review dimensions (configured in `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml`):\n145: \n146: | Dimension | Focus | Key Questions |\n147: |-----------|-------|---------------|\n148: | **Correctness** | Logic, behavior, error handling | Behavior matches claims? Edge cases handled? |\n149: | **Security** | Vulnerabilities, exposure, trust boundaries | Inputs validated? Credentials exposed? |\n150: | **Spec-Alignment / Traceability** | Spec vs. implementation fidelity | Code matches spec.md? Planned items present? |\n151: | **Completeness / Maintainability** | Coverage, dead code, documentation | TODOs resolved? Code self-documenting? |\n152: \n153: ### Lifecycle + Reducer Contract\n154: \n155: Review mode is lineage-aware.\n156: \n157: - **Lifecycle modes:** `new`, `resume`, and `restart`.\n158: - **Required lineage fields:** `sessionId`, `parentSessionId`, `lineageMode`, `generation`, `continuedFromRun`, and `releaseReadinessState`.\n159: - **Reducer contract:** consumes the latest JSONL delta, the new iteration file, and prior reduced state, then emits finding registry, dashboard metrics, and strategy updates.\n160: \n161: ### Severity Classification\n162: \n163: | Severity | Criteria | Blocking |\n164: |----------|----------|---------|\n165: | **P0** | Correctness failure, security vulnerability, spec contradiction | Yes, blocks PASS verdict |\n166: | **P1** | Degraded behavior, incomplete implementation, missing validation | Conditional, triggers CONDITIONAL verdict |\n167: | **P2** | Style, naming, minor improvements, documentation gaps | No, PASS with advisories |\n168: \n169: P0 and P1 also follow the reach calibration in `sk-code-review`'s `references/review-core.md` §2.\n170: A blocking finding names an input or call path the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input.\n171: \"Missing validation\" is P1 only when the unvalidated input is in reach. Validation the code never claimed, for input outside its stated contract, is P2 hardening.\n172: At a trust boundary every value the outside can send is in reach.\n173: \n174: The scale has exactly three tiers, and no fourth is reserved.\n175: A severity outside P0/P1/P2 is not a fourth tier. That covers a `P3` inherited from an older four-level convention, and any unlisted word.\n176: Collapse it to the tier its impact actually matches (a `P3` becomes `P2`) and keep the original rating visible in the finding, so the rating is converted rather than lost.\n177: An unconverted out-of-scale severity reaches the cross-lineage merge unranked, where it sorts below every P2 and cannot raise the merged verdict.\n178: \n179: ### Verdicts\n180: \n181: | Verdict | Condition |\n182: |---------|-----------|\n183: | **PASS** | No P0/P1 findings, P2 findings recorded as advisories (`hasAdvisories: true`) |\n184: | **CONDITIONAL** | P1 findings present, remediation plan included in report |\n185: | **FAIL** | Any P0 finding confirmed after adversarial self-check |\n186: \n187: ### Acceptance-Coverage Signal\n188: \n189: When the review target is a spec folder, deep review reflects the `AC_COVERAGE` validation signal in synthesis for Level 2+ folders.\n190: The signal applies only once `checklist.md` exists and `implementation-summary.md` is in-progress or later. Level 1 folders and fresh scaffolds are exempt.\n191: It is advisory while the validation rule stays INFO/default-on (non-blocking, and `RULE_STATUS` stays `pass`).\n192: It can add traceability context and planning-seed work, but must not alter the iteration final-line contract below unless a later enforcement rollout explicitly changes severity.\n193: \n194: ### Iteration Final-Line Contract (MANDATORY)\n195: \n196: Every `iteration-NNN.md` MUST end with exactly one of the following plain-text lines as the **absolute final line** (no trailing whitespace, no variation), and every review MUST emit exactly one parseable verdict:\n197: \n198: ```\n199: Review verdict: PASS\n200: ```\n201: \n202: ```\n203: Review verdict: CONDITIONAL\n204: ```\n205: \n206: ```\n207: Review verdict: FAIL\n208: ```\n209: \n210: **Mapping rule:** PASS if no P0 or P1 findings in this iteration. CONDITIONAL if any P1 (but no P0) findings. FAIL if any P0 findings. P2-only findings → PASS.\n211: \n212: **VERDICT_LOCK:** Any confirmed active P0 forces the exact final line `Review verdict: FAIL` -- never relabel that state as conditional, partial, mixed, or advisory, and truncated/partial output is not a valid substitute for the final line. An optional advisory `riskScore` may appear in narratives/JSONL for relative risk calibration but never changes the `PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL` mapping.\n213: \n214: Downstream automation (including the synthesis phase and CI gate parser) parses this final line via exact string match, do not vary the format.\n215: \n216: **What the check proves:** the post-dispatch verifier validates the *shape* of this line, and nothing more: exactly one well-formed verdict line, and it is final.\n217: It does not read findings, so a `PASS` written above active P1 findings satisfies it, and no parser here makes the line true.\n218: The verdict a release decision reads is recomputed from the findings registry.\n219: In a fan-out run the cross-lineage merge is the authoritative rollup, where any active P0 becomes a merged FAIL.\n220: Treat the final line as the leaf's self-report and the registry as the record.\n221: \n222: ### Executor Selection Contract\n223: \n224: Executor settings are owned by the YAML workflow and rendered prompt pack -- never hand-dispatch review iterations; each iteration stays LEAF-only and produces the required markdown plus JSONL delta. Full contract (per-iteration invariants, failure modes, JSONL audit field, config surface/precedence, TrustState surface): `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n225: \n226: ---\n227: \n228: ## 4. RULES\n229: \n230: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n231: \n232: 1. Read JSONL and strategy before review action.\n233: 2. Review one dimension per iteration and write findings to `iteration-NNN.md`.\n234: 3. Append JSONL with severity counts, finding detail, and `newInfoRatio`.\n235: 4. Cite every finding with `[SOURCE: file:line]`. Reject inference-only findings.\n236: 5. Re-read cited code before recording any P0.\n237: 6. Keep target files read-only.\n238: 7. Use `generate-context.js` for continuity saves. **Owner**: the YAML workflow (`deep_review_{auto,confirm}.yaml`) calls it at the save phase, not the reducer (`scripts/reduce-state.cjs`) directly — don't expect continuity-save side effects from the reducer alone.\n239: 8. Emit setup `BINDING:` lines before workflow output.\n240: 9. Refuse nested dispatch with: `REFUSE: nested Task tool dispatch is forbidden for LEAF agents. Returning partial findings instead.`\n241: \n242: ### ⛔ NEVER\n243: \n244: 1. **Dispatch sub-agents**, `@deep-review` is LEAF-only. It cannot dispatch additional agents. When dispatch is requested, use the canonical REFUSE wording (ALWAYS rule 9).\n245: 2. **Hold findings in context**, Write everything to iteration files. Context is discarded after each dispatch.\n246: 3. **Exceed TCB**, Target 8-11 tool calls per iteration (max 12). Breadth over depth per cycle.\n247: 4. **Ask the user**, Autonomous execution. The agent makes best-judgment decisions without pausing.\n248: 5. **Skip convergence checks**, Every iteration must be evaluated against convergence criteria before the next dispatch.\n249: 6. **Modify config after init**, review parameters in `deep-review-config.json` are read-only after initialization; the terminal `step_update_config_status` flip to `status: complete` is the one permitted write.\n250: 7. **Modify files under review**, The review loop is observation-only. No code changes during audit.\n251: 8. **Use WebFetch**, Review is code-only. No external resource fetching is permitted.\n252: 9. **Implement fixes during review**, Report findings only. Implementation is a separate follow-up step.\n253: \n254: ### Iteration Status Enum\n255: \n256: `complete | timeout | error | stuck | insight`\n257: \n258: - `insight`: Low newInfoRatio but important finding that changes the verdict trajectory.\n259: \n260: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n261: \n262: 1. **3+ consecutive timeouts**, Infrastructure issue. Pause loop and report to user.\n263: 2. **State file corruption**, Cannot reconstruct iteration history from JSONL or iteration files.\n264: 3. **All dimensions covered with P0 findings remaining**, Human sign-off required before shipping.\n265: 4. **Security vulnerabilities discovered in production code**, Escalate immediately. Do not defer to report synthesis.\n266: 5. **All recovery tiers exhausted**, No automatic recovery path remaining in convergence protocol.\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 5. REFERENCES\n271: \n272: The router discovers reference, asset and script docs dynamically.\n273: Start with these, in order:\n274: \n275: 1. `references/protocol/quick-reference.md`\n276: 2. `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`\n277: 3. `references/convergence/convergence.md`\n278: 4. `references/convergence/convergence-signals.md`\n279: 5. `references/state/state-format.md`\n280: 6. `references/state/state-outputs.md`\n281: 7. `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`\n282: 8. `assets/deep-review-dashboard.md`\n283: 9. `assets/deep-review-strategy.md`\n284: \n285: Then load task-specific resources from `references/`, templates from `assets/`, and automation from `scripts/` when present.\n286: \n287: Scripts: `scripts/reduce-state.cjs`, `scripts/runtime-capabilities.cjs`.\n288: \n289: Detailed contracts:\n290: \n291: - `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`: executor invariants, failure modes and config surface.\n292: - `references/protocol/loop-state-and-gates.md`: state transitions, error handling and STOP-decision gates.\n293: - `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`: the full loop-completion, quality-gate and validation-success checklist.\n294: - `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`: two-tier content-hash dedup.\n295: \n296: ---\n297: \n298: ## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n299: \n300: A review loop is complete only when convergence and every quality gate agree.\n301: All of these must hold:\n302: \n303: - The composite stop score clears `compositeStopScore`, or `maxIterations` is hit without a false-positive STOP.\n304: - Every configured dimension plus the required traceability protocols (`spec_code`, `checklist_evidence`) has at least one full iteration of coverage.\n305: - All canonical state files exist and parse cleanly: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, `deep-review-strategy.md` and `deep-review-dashboard.md` (a fan-out run keeps one per lineage instead), plus one `iterations/iteration-NNN.md` per dispatched iteration.\n306: - `review-report.md` carries all 9 core sections plus the conditional `## Resource Map Coverage Gate`.\n307: - Continuity is saved via `generate-context.js`.\n308: \n309: Nine binary quality gates must all pass before STOP is legal:\n310: \n311: 1. Config validity and lineage match.\n312: 2. Strategy initialization completeness.\n313: 3. State and registry consistency.\n314: 4. Iteration completeness (markdown plus JSONL delta).\n315: 5. Severity-field coverage on every finding (`severity`/`category`/`file:line`/`content_hash`).\n316: 6. The advisory-only `riskScore` never gating verdict logic.\n317: 7. Adversarial P0 replay.\n318: 8. Dimension and protocol coverage stability.\n319: 9. Acceptance coverage: the advisory `AC_COVERAGE` signal when the spec-folder lifecycle predicate is active.\n320: \n321: The security-sensitive override also applies when the target touches security, path handling, env precedence, schema boundaries, persistence or shared policy (`minStabilizationPasses=2` plus fix-completeness replay).\n322: Full gate-by-gate criteria and rationale: `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`.\n323: \n324: Validate a completed run with `skill_advisor.py \"run a deep review loop\" --threshold 0.8` (skill still surfaces) and `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict` (exits 0).\n325: \n326: ---\n327: \n328: ## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n329: \n330: ### Framework Integration\n331: \n332: This skill operates within the behavioral framework defined in the active runtime's root doc (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md).\n333: \n334: Key integrations:\n335: - **Gate 2**: Skill routing via `skill_advisor.py` (keywords: deep review, convergence review, iterative review)\n336: - **Gate 3**: File modifications require spec folder question per the root doc Gate 3. The spec folder determines the `{spec_folder}/review/` state packet location\n337: - **Continuity**: `/speckit:resume` is the operator-facing recovery surface, resolving context in the order `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`. During review, the agent writes iteration, strategy, and JSONL state; after synthesis, run `generate-context.js` to write canonical packet continuity\n338: - **Command**: `/deep:review` is the primary invocation point\n339: \n340: ### Code Graph Integration\n341: \n342: The review loop consumes the shared runtime graph and its strict mode-gate validator; packet-specific convergence and state rules remain in this skill.\n343: \n344: ### Related Skills\n345: \n346: Related skills: `deep-research` for investigation loops, `sk-code`'s code-review mode for single-pass review doctrine, and `system-spec-kit` for command-owned state and continuity saves.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md, lines 1-346\n1: ---\n2: name: deep-review\n3: description: \"Autonomous iterative code-review loop with externalized state, convergence detection, P0/P1/P2 findings, fresh context per pass.\"\n4: argument-hint: \"[target] [:auto|:confirm] [--max-iterations=N] [--convergence=N] [--stop-policy=convergence|max-iterations]\"\n5: version: 1.11.14.0\n6: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, Task]\n7: ---\n8: <!-- Note: Task is for the command executor (loop management); @deep-review agent is LEAF-only (no Task). No WebFetch: review is code-only. -->\n9: \n10: # Autonomous Deep Review Loop\n11: \n12: Iterative code review and quality auditing protocol with fresh context per iteration, externalized state, convergence detection, and severity-weighted findings (P0/P1/P2).\n13: \n14: Runtime path resolution:\n15: - OpenCode/Copilot runtime: `.opencode/agents/*.md`\n16: - Claude runtime: `.claude/agents/*.md`\n17: \n18: Convergence threshold semantics and sibling-parity notes (deep-review 0.10 vs deep-research 0.05 vs deep-ai-council 0.20) live in `references/convergence/convergence.md` §1 under \"Threshold Semantics and Sibling Parity\".\n19: \n20: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n21: \n22: ### When to Use This Skill\n23: \n24: Use this skill when:\n25: - Code quality audit requiring multiple rounds across different review dimensions\n26: - Spec folder validation requiring cross-reference checks between docs and implementation\n27: - Release readiness check before shipping a feature or component\n28: - Finding misalignments between spec documents and actual code\n29: - Verifying cross-references across documentation, agents, commands, and code\n30: - Iterative review where each dimension's findings inform subsequent dimensions\n31: - Unattended or overnight audit sessions\n32: \n33: ### When NOT to Use\n34: \n35: - Simple single-pass code review (use `sk-code`'s code-review mode instead)\n36: - Known issues that just need fixing (go directly to implementation)\n37: - Implementation tasks (use `sk-code` or `/speckit:implement`)\n38: - Quick one-file checks (use direct Grep/Read)\n39: - Fewer than 2 review dimensions needed (single-pass suffices)\n40: \n41: ### FORBIDDEN INVOCATION PATTERNS\n42: \n43: This skill is invoked EXCLUSIVELY through the `/deep:review` command. The command's YAML workflow owns state, dispatch, and convergence.\n44: \n45: **NEVER:**\n46: - Write a custom bash/shell dispatcher to parallelize iterations (ad-hoc shell fan-out)\n47: - Invoke any CLI executor (cli-codex, cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-cursor, cli-devin, cli-pi, cli-deepseek-harness) directly in a loop to simulate iterations\n48: - Manually write iteration prompts to `/tmp` and dispatch them via `copilot -p`\n49: - Dispatch the `@deep-review` LEAF agent via the Task tool for iteration loops (the agent is LEAF, a single iteration, and MUST be driven by the command's workflow)\n50: - Skip the state machine: `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-config.json`, `deltas/`, `prompts/`, `logs/`\n51: - Manage iteration state outside the resolved local review packet under `{spec_folder}/review/`\n52: \n53: **COMMAND-DRIVEN FAN-OUT IS SUPPORTED:** use `--executor`/`--executors`/`--concurrency` flags on `/deep:review`. The command's YAML `step_fanout_spawn` owns multi-lineage dispatch; `fanout-merge.cjs` applies strongest-restriction (any lineage active P0 → merged FAIL). This is not ad-hoc shell dispatch — it is the canonical fan-out path. Intra-lineage wave orchestration remains deferred.\n54: \n55: **ALWAYS:**\n56: - Invoke via `/deep:review :auto` or `/deep:review :confirm`\n57: - Let the command's YAML workflow own dispatch (auto: `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml`)\n58: - Record each leaf iteration through the append gateway (`runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --mode review --run-directory <spec folder> --event-json <file>`) — the gateway is the SINGLE state-log writer: it authorizes, fences, and receipts the write, then refreshes `{state_paths.state_log}` from the ledger\n59: - Treat `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` as the reducer for DERIVED artifacts only (finding registry, dashboard metrics, strategy updates); it never writes the state log\n60: - Require every iteration to produce BOTH the markdown narrative AND the JSONL delta (dispatch scripts must fail if either is missing)\n61: - Use `resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review')` from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/shared/review-research-paths.cjs` to locate the canonical review root\n62: \n63: ### Trigger Phrases\n64: \n65: - \"review code quality\" / \"audit this code\"\n66: - \"audit spec folder\" / \"validate spec completeness\"\n67: - \"release readiness check\" / \"pre-release review\"\n68: - \"find misalignments\" (between spec and implementation)\n69: - \"verify cross-references\" (across docs and code)\n70: - \"deep review\" / \"iterative review\" / \"review loop\"\n71: - \"quality audit\" / \"convergence detection\"\n72: \n73: ### Keyword Triggers\n74: \n75: `deep review`, `convergence review`, `iterative review`, `review loop`, `release readiness`, `spec folder review`, `convergence detection`, `quality audit`, `find misalignments`, `verify cross-references`, `pre-release review`, `audit spec folder`\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n80: \n81: The `system-deep-loop` hub routes an iterative review loop here, and its `review` mode signal selects this packet.\n82: The signal fires when a request names a deep review, a convergence review, a review loop, a release readiness check or a spec-folder audit.\n83: The loop runs only through `/deep:review`, whose workflow owns state, dispatch and convergence.\n84: \n85: `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` loads first on every invocation.\n86: The review phase named by the dispatch context then selects the loop protocol, the convergence documents or the state documents.\n87: \n88: The routing pseudocode in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) is the one route from a request to a resource set.\n89: A request whose intent scores stay under the threshold returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with a disambiguation checklist, never a silent default.\n90: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n95: \n96: ### Architecture\n97: \n98: `/deep:review` owns the loop. The YAML workflow initializes state, dispatches one LEAF review iteration at a time, evaluates convergence, synthesizes `review-report.md`, and saves continuity. The LEAF agent reads state, reviews one dimension, writes `iteration-NNN.md`, updates strategy, and appends JSONL. Full 4-phase lifecycle: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n99: \n100: ### Resource Map Coverage Gate\n101: \n102: When `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init, deep review treats it as a mandatory audit input and does four things:\n103: \n104: - Persists `resource_map_present` in config.\n105: - Seeds `Known Context` with a snapshot of the map.\n106: - Has at least one iteration audit `target_files` coverage against the map: touched, not-touched with `expected-by-scope`-versus-`gap`, and absent-from-map, with findings tagged `resource-map-coverage`.\n107: - Has synthesis add the `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` report section and a converged `resource-map.md`. Disable this with `--no-resource-map`.\n108: \n109: Absent at init, the gate and section are skipped without failing the loop.\n110: Full sequencing: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` (init 9a, iteration 3b, synthesis 4). Schema: `references/state/state-format.md`.\n111: \n112: ### Bounded Context Snapshot Replacement\n113: \n114: During initialization, capture a bounded, pointer-based context snapshot in `deep-review-strategy.md` `Known Context` before the first dimension runs.\n115: The snapshot holds:\n116: \n117: - Target pointers: files, specs, symbols and resource-map entries.\n118: - The claimed behavior and acceptance criteria to verify.\n119: - Reuse and convention pointers.\n120: - Risk areas, missing context, stale-graph caveats and out-of-scope areas.\n121: \n122: Do not create a context-loop report or widen scope beyond the declared target.\n123: Use `@context` for quick retrieval, and `/speckit:plan` only when findings require implementation planning.\n124: \n125: ### State Packet Location\n126: \n127: The review state packet always lives under the target spec's local `review/` folder.\n128: Root-spec targets use `{spec_folder}/review/` directly.\n129: Child-phase and sub-phase targets use **flat-first** placement: the first run writes flat, and a `pt-NN` sibling packet is allocated only when prior content already exists for a different target.\n130: Full resolution rule and worked examples: `references/state/state-format.md` §1.\n131: \n132: Core artifacts: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-strategy.md`, `deep-review-dashboard.md`, `.deep-review-pause`, `resource-map.md`, `review-report.md`, and `iterations/iteration-NNN.md`.\n133: \n134: The loop writes `review/review-report.md` itself at synthesis rather than scaffolding it through `create.sh --level review`.\n135: The report is assembled from the findings registry and the JSONL state, so its content exists only once the loop has run, and a scaffolded skeleton would be overwritten whole.\n136: `create.sh --level review` exists for a review packet authored by hand, and its template carries the same nine sections the loop emits.\n137: \n138: ### Core Innovation: Fresh Context Per Iteration\n139: \n140: Each agent dispatch gets a fresh context window; state continuity comes from files, not memory, preventing accumulated findings from biasing later dimensions. Init writes config/strategy/JSONL; each loop reads state, checks convergence, dispatches one dimension, records findings, and reduces state; synthesis compiles the report and saves continuity.\n141: \n142: ### Review Dimensions\n143: \n144: The four primary review dimensions (configured in `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml`):\n145: \n146: | Dimension | Focus | Key Questions |\n147: |-----------|-------|---------------|\n148: | **Correctness** | Logic, behavior, error handling | Behavior matches claims? Edge cases handled? |\n149: | **Security** | Vulnerabilities, exposure, trust boundaries | Inputs validated? Credentials exposed? |\n150: | **Spec-Alignment / Traceability** | Spec vs. implementation fidelity | Code matches spec.md? Planned items present? |\n151: | **Completeness / Maintainability** | Coverage, dead code, documentation | TODOs resolved? Code self-documenting? |\n152: \n153: ### Lifecycle + Reducer Contract\n154: \n155: Review mode is lineage-aware.\n156: \n157: - **Lifecycle modes:** `new`, `resume`, and `restart`.\n158: - **Required lineage fields:** `sessionId`, `parentSessionId`, `lineageMode`, `generation`, `continuedFromRun`, and `releaseReadinessState`.\n159: - **Reducer contract:** consumes the latest JSONL delta, the new iteration file, and prior reduced state, then emits finding registry, dashboard metrics, and strategy updates.\n160: \n161: ### Severity Classification\n162: \n163: | Severity | Criteria | Blocking |\n164: |----------|----------|---------|\n165: | **P0** | Correctness failure, security vulnerability, spec contradiction | Yes, blocks PASS verdict |\n166: | **P1** | Degraded behavior, incomplete implementation, missing validation | Conditional, triggers CONDITIONAL verdict |\n167: | **P2** | Style, naming, minor improvements, documentation gaps | No, PASS with advisories |\n168: \n169: P0 and P1 also follow the reach calibration in `sk-code-review`'s `references/review-core.md` §2.\n170: A blocking finding names an input or call path the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input.\n171: \"Missing validation\" is P1 only when the unvalidated input is in reach. Validation the code never claimed, for input outside its stated contract, is P2 hardening.\n172: At a trust boundary every value the outside can send is in reach.\n173: \n174: The scale has exactly three tiers, and no fourth is reserved.\n175: A severity outside P0/P1/P2 is not a fourth tier. That covers a `P3` inherited from an older four-level convention, and any unlisted word.\n176: Collapse it to the tier its impact actually matches (a `P3` becomes `P2`) and keep the original rating visible in the finding, so the rating is converted rather than lost.\n177: An unconverted out-of-scale severity reaches the cross-lineage merge unranked, where it sorts below every P2 and cannot raise the merged verdict.\n178: \n179: ### Verdicts\n180: \n181: | Verdict | Condition |\n182: |---------|-----------|\n183: | **PASS** | No P0/P1 findings, P2 findings recorded as advisories (`hasAdvisories: true`) |\n184: | **CONDITIONAL** | P1 findings present, remediation plan included in report |\n185: | **FAIL** | Any P0 finding confirmed after adversarial self-check |\n186: \n187: ### Acceptance-Coverage Signal\n188: \n189: When the review target is a spec folder, deep review reflects the `AC_COVERAGE` validation signal in synthesis for Level 2+ folders.\n190: The signal applies only once `checklist.md` exists and `implementation-summary.md` is in-progress or later. Level 1 folders and fresh scaffolds are exempt.\n191: It is advisory while the validation rule stays INFO/default-on (non-blocking, and `RULE_STATUS` stays `pass`).\n192: It can add traceability context and planning-seed work, but must not alter the iteration final-line contract below unless a later enforcement rollout explicitly changes severity.\n193: \n194: ### Iteration Final-Line Contract (MANDATORY)\n195: \n196: Every `iteration-NNN.md` MUST end with exactly one of the following plain-text lines as the **absolute final line** (no trailing whitespace, no variation), and every review MUST emit exactly one parseable verdict:\n197: \n198: ```\n199: Review verdict: PASS\n200: ```\n201: \n202: ```\n203: Review verdict: CONDITIONAL\n204: ```\n205: \n206: ```\n207: Review verdict: FAIL\n208: ```\n209: \n210: **Mapping rule:** PASS if no P0 or P1 findings in this iteration. CONDITIONAL if any P1 (but no P0) findings. FAIL if any P0 findings. P2-only findings → PASS.\n211: \n212: **VERDICT_LOCK:** Any confirmed active P0 forces the exact final line `Review verdict: FAIL` -- never relabel that state as conditional, partial, mixed, or advisory, and truncated/partial output is not a valid substitute for the final line. An optional advisory `riskScore` may appear in narratives/JSONL for relative risk calibration but never changes the `PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL` mapping.\n213: \n214: Downstream automation (including the synthesis phase and CI gate parser) parses this final line via exact string match, do not vary the format.\n215: \n216: **What the check proves:** the post-dispatch verifier validates the *shape* of this line, and nothing more: exactly one well-formed verdict line, and it is final.\n217: It does not read findings, so a `PASS` written above active P1 findings satisfies it, and no parser here makes the line true.\n218: The verdict a release decision reads is recomputed from the findings registry.\n219: In a fan-out run the cross-lineage merge is the authoritative rollup, where any active P0 becomes a merged FAIL.\n220: Treat the final line as the leaf's self-report and the registry as the record.\n221: \n222: ### Executor Selection Contract\n223: \n224: Executor settings are owned by the YAML workflow and rendered prompt pack -- never hand-dispatch review iterations; each iteration stays LEAF-only and produces the required markdown plus JSONL delta. Full contract (per-iteration invariants, failure modes, JSONL audit field, config surface/precedence, TrustState surface): `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n225: \n226: ---\n227: \n228: ## 4. RULES\n229: \n230: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n231: \n232: 1. Read JSONL and strategy before review action.\n233: 2. Review one dimension per iteration and write findings to `iteration-NNN.md`.\n234: 3. Append JSONL with severity counts, finding detail, and `newInfoRatio`.\n235: 4. Cite every finding with `[SOURCE: file:line]`. Reject inference-only findings.\n236: 5. Re-read cited code before recording any P0.\n237: 6. Keep target files read-only.\n238: 7. Use `generate-context.js` for continuity saves. **Owner**: the YAML workflow (`deep_review_{auto,confirm}.yaml`) calls it at the save phase, not the reducer (`scripts/reduce-state.cjs`) directly — don't expect continuity-save side effects from the reducer alone.\n239: 8. Emit setup `BINDING:` lines before workflow output.\n240: 9. Refuse nested dispatch with: `REFUSE: nested Task tool dispatch is forbidden for LEAF agents. Returning partial findings instead.`\n241: \n242: ### ⛔ NEVER\n243: \n244: 1. **Dispatch sub-agents**, `@deep-review` is LEAF-only. It cannot dispatch additional agents. When dispatch is requested, use the canonical REFUSE wording (ALWAYS rule 9).\n245: 2. **Hold findings in context**, Write everything to iteration files. Context is discarded after each dispatch.\n246: 3. **Exceed TCB**, Target 8-11 tool calls per iteration (max 12). Breadth over depth per cycle.\n247: 4. **Ask the user**, Autonomous execution. The agent makes best-judgment decisions without pausing.\n248: 5. **Skip convergence checks**, Every iteration must be evaluated against convergence criteria before the next dispatch.\n249: 6. **Modify config after init**, review parameters in `deep-review-config.json` are read-only after initialization; the terminal `step_update_config_status` flip to `status: complete` is the one permitted write.\n250: 7. **Modify files under review**, The review loop is observation-only. No code changes during audit.\n251: 8. **Use WebFetch**, Review is code-only. No external resource fetching is permitted.\n252: 9. **Implement fixes during review**, Report findings only. Implementation is a separate follow-up step.\n253: \n254: ### Iteration Status Enum\n255: \n256: `complete | timeout | error | stuck | insight`\n257: \n258: - `insight`: Low newInfoRatio but important finding that changes the verdict trajectory.\n259: \n260: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n261: \n262: 1. **3+ consecutive timeouts**, Infrastructure issue. Pause loop and report to user.\n263: 2. **State file corruption**, Cannot reconstruct iteration history from JSONL or iteration files.\n264: 3. **All dimensions covered with P0 findings remaining**, Human sign-off required before shipping.\n265: 4. **Security vulnerabilities discovered in production code**, Escalate immediately. Do not defer to report synthesis.\n266: 5. **All recovery tiers exhausted**, No automatic recovery path remaining in convergence protocol.\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 5. REFERENCES\n271: \n272: The router discovers reference, asset and script docs dynamically.\n273: Start with these, in order:\n274: \n275: 1. `references/protocol/quick-reference.md`\n276: 2. `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`\n277: 3. `references/convergence/convergence.md`\n278: 4. `references/convergence/convergence-signals.md`\n279: 5. `references/state/state-format.md`\n280: 6. `references/state/state-outputs.md`\n281: 7. `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`\n282: 8. `assets/deep-review-dashboard.md`\n283: 9. `assets/deep-review-strategy.md`\n284: \n285: Then load task-specific resources from `references/`, templates from `assets/`, and automation from `scripts/` when present.\n286: \n287: Scripts: `scripts/reduce-state.cjs`, `scripts/runtime-capabilities.cjs`.\n288: \n289: Detailed contracts:\n290: \n291: - `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`: executor invariants, failure modes and config surface.\n292: - `references/protocol/loop-state-and-gates.md`: state transitions, error handling and STOP-decision gates.\n293: - `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`: the full loop-completion, quality-gate and validation-success checklist.\n294: - `references/state/state-reducer-registry.md`: two-tier content-hash dedup.\n295: \n296: ---\n297: \n298: ## 6. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n299: \n300: A review loop is complete only when convergence and every quality gate agree.\n301: All of these must hold:\n302: \n303: - The composite stop score clears `compositeStopScore`, or `maxIterations` is hit without a false-positive STOP.\n304: - Every configured dimension plus the required traceability protocols (`spec_code`, `checklist_evidence`) has at least one full iteration of coverage.\n305: - All canonical state files exist and parse cleanly: `deep-review-config.json`, `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, `deep-review-strategy.md` and `deep-review-dashboard.md` (a fan-out run keeps one per lineage instead), plus one `iterations/iteration-NNN.md` per dispatched iteration.\n306: - `review-report.md` carries all 9 core sections plus the conditional `## Resource Map Coverage Gate`.\n307: - Continuity is saved via `generate-context.js`.\n308: \n309: Nine binary quality gates must all pass before STOP is legal:\n310: \n311: 1. Config validity and lineage match.\n312: 2. Strategy initialization completeness.\n313: 3. State and registry consistency.\n314: 4. Iteration completeness (markdown plus JSONL delta).\n315: 5. Severity-field coverage on every finding (`severity`/`category`/`file:line`/`content_hash`).\n316: 6. The advisory-only `riskScore` never gating verdict logic.\n317: 7. Adversarial P0 replay.\n318: 8. Dimension and protocol coverage stability.\n319: 9. Acceptance coverage: the advisory `AC_COVERAGE` signal when the spec-folder lifecycle predicate is active.\n320: \n321: The security-sensitive override also applies when the target touches security, path handling, env precedence, schema boundaries, persistence or shared policy (`minStabilizationPasses=2` plus fix-completeness replay).\n322: Full gate-by-gate criteria and rationale: `references/protocol/completion-criteria.md`.\n323: \n324: Validate a completed run with `skill_advisor.py \"run a deep review loop\" --threshold 0.8` (skill still surfaces) and `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict` (exits 0).\n325: \n326: ---\n327: \n328: ## 7. INTEGRATION POINTS\n329: \n330: ### Framework Integration\n331: \n332: This skill operates within the behavioral framework defined in the active runtime's root doc (CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md).\n333: \n334: Key integrations:\n335: - **Gate 2**: Skill routing via `skill_advisor.py` (keywords: deep review, convergence review, iterative review)\n336: - **Gate 3**: File modifications require spec folder question per the root doc Gate 3. The spec folder determines the `{spec_folder}/review/` state packet location\n337: - **Continuity**: `/speckit:resume` is the operator-facing recovery surface, resolving context in the order `handover.md -> _memory.continuity -> spec docs`. During review, the agent writes iteration, strategy, and JSONL state; after synthesis, run `generate-context.js` to write canonical packet continuity\n338: - **Command**: `/deep:review` is the primary invocation point\n339: \n340: ### Code Graph Integration\n341: \n342: The review loop consumes the shared runtime graph and its strict mode-gate validator; packet-specific convergence and state rules remain in this skill.\n343: \n344: ### Related Skills\n345: \n346: Related skills: `deep-research` for investigation loops, `sk-code`'s code-review mode for single-pass review doctrine, and `system-spec-kit` for command-owned state and continuity saves."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794073867463,"end":1794073868306}}}}
